---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Cmd from '../../components/ui/Cmd.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../site';

const TOC = [
  { id: 'prerequisites', label: 'Prerequisites' },
  { id: 'get-the-code', label: 'Get the code' },
  { id: 'point-it-at-a-model', label: 'Point it at a model' },
  { id: 'run-it', label: 'Run it' },
  { id: 'flags', label: 'Flags' },
  { id: 'try-it-on-a-real-bug', label: 'Try it on a real bug' },
  { id: 'providers-that-work', label: 'Providers that work' },
  { id: 'server-installs', label: 'Server installs' },
  { id: 'exposing-it', label: 'Exposing it' },
];

const FLAGS = [
  { flag: '--help', meaning: 'Every flag the binary takes, as built.' },
  { flag: '--host', meaning: 'Bind address. Overrides KARUCODE_HOST.' },
  { flag: '--port', meaning: 'Port. Overrides KARUCODE_PORT; the default is 8787.' },
  { flag: '--workspace', meaning: 'The default workspace root. Overrides KARUCODE_WORKSPACE.' },
  { flag: '--data-dir', meaning: 'Where the SQLite database and plugin data live.' },
  { flag: '--config', meaning: 'Path to the TOML config file. Overrides KARUCODE_CONFIG.' },
];

const PROVIDERS = [
  { name: 'LiteLLM proxy', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1' },
  { name: 'vLLM', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1' },
  { name: 'Ollama', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1' },
  { name: 'llama.cpp server', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1' },
  { name: 'OpenRouter', url: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1' },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Quick start"
  description="Clone karucode, point it at an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, run the server, and give it a task."
  path="/docs/quickstart/"
  section="Docs"
  lede="Clone it, point it at a model, run a task."
  toc={TOC}
  edit={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/README.md`}
  prev={{ href: '/docs/', title: 'Overview' }}
  next={{ href: '/docs/configuration/', title: 'Configuration' }}
>
  <p>
    Most of the time in this page goes into the first <code>cargo build</code>. You need a machine
    that can compile Rust, and an OpenAI-compatible endpoint — a local gateway, a hosted API, or a
    model server you already run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rust 1.89+</strong> — <code>rustup</code> is the usual way to get it.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Node 22.12+</strong> — only to build the frontend. <code>cargo build</code> runs the
      frontend build for you; nothing needs Node at runtime.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong> — the primary target. Other Unixes should work.</li>
    <li>
      Optional: <code>git</code>, and <code>ripgrep</code> — there is a pure-Rust search fallback if
      you don’t have it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That is the whole list. There is no cloud account, no signup and no telemetry: the binary talks
    to the endpoint you give it and to nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="get-the-code">Get the code</h2>
  <Cmd label="shell" wrap command="git clone https://github.com/karutoil/karucode && cd karucode" />
  <p>
    The repository holds the server, the embedded UI and the packaging files. Nothing needs
    installing at runtime — the binary is the deployment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="point-it-at-a-model">Point it at a model</h2>
  <p>
    Three variables. <code>KARUCODE_BASE_URL</code> is the gateway, <code>KARUCODE_API_KEY</code> is
    what it authenticates with (it falls back to <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>), and
    <code>KARUCODE_MODEL</code> is the model id you want.
  </p>
  <Cmd
    label="shell"
    prompt=""
    wrap
    command={`export KARUCODE_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1
export KARUCODE_API_KEY=sk-...
export KARUCODE_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini`}
  />
  <p>
    <code>base_url</code> is a prefix, not a full URL: karucode appends
    <code>/chat/completions</code> to it. Point it at the <code>/v1</code> root of your gateway, not
    at a single endpoint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="run-it">Run it</h2>
  <Cmd
    label="shell"
    wrap
    command="cargo run --release -p kc-server -- --workspace /path/to/your/project"
  />
  <p>
    <code>cargo build</code> also builds and embeds the frontend, so that one build is the whole
    build. Then open <a href="http://127.0.0.1:8787">http://127.0.0.1:8787</a>, create a session,
    type a task, and approve the commands you want it to run.
  </p>
  <p>
    The default approval mode is <code>ask</code>: shell commands and file writes wait for you. You
    can answer allow-once, allow-for-session or deny, and every decision is recorded.
  </p>

  <h2 id="flags">Flags</h2>
  <p>
    A flag beats the environment variable of the same name for the process it starts, which is the
    quickest way to run two instances with two different workspaces.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Flag</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          FLAGS.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{f.flag}</td>
              <td>{f.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="try-it-on-a-real-bug">Try it on a real bug</h2>
  <p>
    <code>examples/demo-app</code> in the repo is a dependency-free Node project with a planted bug:
    <code>add()</code> subtracts instead of adding, so two of its five tests fail. Start the server
    against it:
  </p>
  <Cmd label="shell" wrap command="cargo run --release -p kc-server -- --workspace examples/demo-app" />
  <p>Then give it this prompt:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Run the tests, find the failing cases, fix the bug in src/math.js, and re-run the tests to prove
      it passes.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Watch what it does: it runs the tests, reads the failures, opens the file, edits one function, and
    runs the tests again. You will be asked before the first command and before the write. That
    loop — run, read, change, prove — is the whole product.
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers-that-work">Providers that work</h2>
  <p>
    Anything that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions API with streaming tool calls. These are the
    shapes people point it at most often:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Provider</th>
          <th scope="col">KARUCODE_BASE_URL</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          PROVIDERS.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p.name}</td>
              <td>
                <code>{p.url}</code>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    With Ollama, tool calling depends on the model you pull — a model that cannot emit tool calls
    cannot drive the agent. Provider profiles are install-wide; a session can pin its own model,
    provider and reasoning effort. See <a href="/docs/configuration/">Configuration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="server-installs">Server installs</h2>
  <p>
    For anything long-lived, <code>packaging/</code> in the repo holds a container build and a
    systemd installer. Release tarballs are verified against the release <code>SHA256SUMS</code>
    before install, and nothing is piped from the network into a shell — there is no
    <code>curl | bash</code> installer.
  </p>
  <Cmd
    label="container"
    prompt=""
    wrap
    command={`docker build -t karucode packaging/
docker run --rm -p 127.0.0.1:8787:8787 \\
  -e KARUCODE_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1 \\
  --mount type=bind,source="$PWD",target=/workspace \\
  karucode`}
  />
  <p>And the systemd path, run from an unpacked, verified release tarball:</p>
  <Cmd label="systemd" wrap command="sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS && ./packaging/install.sh" />
  <p>
    The container is also the answer to the next section: it is the isolation boundary karucode
    itself does not provide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exposing-it">Exposing it</h2>
  <Callout kind="danger" title="Not for a public interface as-is">
    <p>
      karucode binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default and refuses a non-loopback bind unless
      <code>KARUCODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code> is set to at least 16 characters. There is no TLS in the
      binary: put a reverse proxy in front of it for remote access.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shell commands run as the server user and are not sandboxed. If the workspace is untrusted, run
      the whole thing inside a container or a VM. <a href="/security/">Security</a> is the full
      version of this page.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Docs>

